            <ArticleTitle>Serum chemerin levels are associated with the presence and extent of coronary artery disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>412-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/MCA.0b013e3283576a60</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The aim of the study was to examine whether serum chemerin levels are associated with the presence and the extent of coronary artery disease (CAD).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIALS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A total of 132 patients with CAD and 56 patients without CAD who underwent coronary angiography for the evaluation of CAD were enrolled in this study. Serum levels of chemerin were measured using an enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Serum chemerin levels were significantly elevated in CAD patients compared with those without CAD. Multivariate logistic regression analysis showed that serum chemerin levels were significantly associated with the presence of CAD. In CAD patients, chemerin was positively correlated with BMI (r=0.274, P=0.001) and triglycerides (r=0.190, P=0.029), and yet correlated with low-density lipoprotein-cholesterol (r=0.228, P=0.008); the association of chemerin with triglycerides and low-density lipoprotein-cholesterol remained significant after adjusting for BMI (P&lt;0.05 and P&lt;0.01). At multiple stepwise regression analysis, serum chemerin levels were an independent predictor of the stenosis score (β=0.193, P=0.034).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Our data suggest that increased chemerin levels are associated with the presence of CAD and that serum chemerin levels may reflect the extent of coronary atherosclerosis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
